WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.498 --> 00:00:02.394
- He's a Russian chess grandmaster

00:00:02.394 --> 00:00:04.906
and former chess world champion.

00:00:04.906 --> 00:00:06.227
He's considered by many to be

00:00:06.227 --> 00:00:08.763
the greatest chess player of all time.

00:00:08.763 --> 00:00:10.221
He became the youngest ever

00:00:10.221 --> 00:00:13.949
undisputed World Chess Champion at age 22.

00:00:13.949 --> 00:00:15.237
He's Garry Kasparov

00:00:15.237 --> 00:00:18.183
and here are his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:18.183 --> 00:00:21.117
(wind howling)
(basketball bouncing)

00:00:21.117 --> 00:00:24.699
- It's very important, and
I also come back to chess,

00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:28.742
to learn how to recognize your
own strengths and weaknesses.

00:00:28.742 --> 00:00:31.873
This is in fact the
vital part of preparation

00:00:31.873 --> 00:00:35.210
for a big match and for anything in life.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:37.351
We always try to study our opponents.

00:00:37.351 --> 00:00:39.870
We always try to study the battlefield.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:42.564
We always try to study
all the circumstances

00:00:42.564 --> 00:00:45.263
surrounding our venture or endeavor.

00:00:45.263 --> 00:00:48.667
But, very often we
forget to study ourself,

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:51.360
and this is very important
because any choice

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.193
of a ground for a battle
or a type of strategy

00:00:56.331 --> 00:00:59.414
you want to employ
depends on our recognition

00:00:59.414 --> 00:01:02.548
of our strengths and weaknesses.

00:01:02.548 --> 00:01:04.688
And this is a process
of constant reevaluation

00:01:04.688 --> 00:01:06.948
because life is changing very fast

00:01:06.948 --> 00:01:10.247
and our opponents, looking at our success,

00:01:10.247 --> 00:01:12.719
studying it, and trying to be smart

00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:14.394
and outsmart us eventually.

00:01:14.394 --> 00:01:17.908
And we can look at the
history and just to find

00:01:17.908 --> 00:01:21.408
a number of situations where the inability

00:01:23.626 --> 00:01:25.853
to recognize strengths and weaknesses

00:01:25.853 --> 00:01:28.270
lead to an eventual disaster.

00:01:30.974 --> 00:01:33.891
(taps chess clock)

00:01:35.534 --> 00:01:39.701
(taps chess clock)
(piece hits table)

00:01:50.761 --> 00:01:54.288
It's up to us, what we want
to do with our success,

00:01:54.288 --> 00:01:58.150
because, you know, eventually
if we are successful

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:01.181
we just have to make a
conscious decision what's next.

00:02:01.181 --> 00:02:03.963
And for me, there was
always a next station

00:02:03.963 --> 00:02:06.783
because it wasn't enough
for me just to win.

00:02:06.783 --> 00:02:08.541
I always wanted to make a difference.

00:02:08.541 --> 00:02:11.389
It could also remind us of
success of Alexander Bell,

00:02:11.389 --> 00:02:14.232
the inventor of telephone,
who had a stack of notebooks

00:02:14.232 --> 00:02:17.893
where he recorded every failed experiment.

00:02:17.893 --> 00:02:22.060
So if you work really hard
you can find the winning spot

00:02:23.808 --> 00:02:27.475
even in the midst of
your numerous failures.

00:02:29.224 --> 00:02:32.692
I think the ex-speaker of
US House of Representatives

00:02:32.692 --> 00:02:37.653
Newt Gingrich hit the right
points when he said that

00:02:37.653 --> 00:02:40.320
perseverance is hard work you do

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:44.788
after you get tired of doing
hard work you already did.

00:02:44.788 --> 00:02:48.336
I think that, again, from my perspective,

00:02:48.336 --> 00:02:52.336
patience is your ability
to also accept failures

00:02:56.539 --> 00:03:00.622
because very often today,
even from kindergarten,

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.451
and of course in schools,
and of course in colleges,

00:03:04.451 --> 00:03:08.363
and of course at the work
place in businesses we hear,

00:03:08.363 --> 00:03:09.546
oh, failure, failure.

00:03:09.546 --> 00:03:11.957
You know, failure's nothing but failure.

00:03:11.957 --> 00:03:16.312
Well, failure is inevitable
step toward success.

00:03:16.312 --> 00:03:20.479
If you don't take a risk, how
can you be sure about rewards?

00:03:22.617 --> 00:03:26.979
Especially if you're
looking for something big.

00:03:26.979 --> 00:03:29.681
You want to do something
that never happened before.

00:03:29.681 --> 00:03:31.446
You're looking for breaks or innovations.

00:03:31.446 --> 00:03:33.936
It's like sort of the Moon project.

00:03:33.936 --> 00:03:37.019
Something that is for the blue skies.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:42.302
It means you have to
enter uncharted waters,

00:03:42.302 --> 00:03:44.620
sort of untested territories.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:47.769
And again, for me, patience is also

00:03:47.769 --> 00:03:51.852
a willingness to accept failure,
defeat, and to try again.

00:03:51.852 --> 00:03:54.897
I learned that, you know,
it's never late to learn.

00:03:54.897 --> 00:03:57.365
So that's why even at age 37

00:03:57.365 --> 00:03:59.030
you can learn from a younger generation

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.449
and that was a very important lesson

00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:04.459
not only for the remaining
five years of my chess career,

00:04:04.459 --> 00:04:08.626
but also for future years,
because I just recognized that,

00:04:10.886 --> 00:04:15.053
I mean, flexibility is a
fundamental quality for success.

00:04:16.126 --> 00:04:20.753
We should learn how to
trust our gut, intuition.

00:04:20.753 --> 00:04:24.466
Because in most of the
stories I looked at,

00:04:24.466 --> 00:04:26.691
and in most of the quotes I collected

00:04:26.691 --> 00:04:29.638
I didn't see people picking up intuition

00:04:29.638 --> 00:04:32.487
as the crucial element of the success.

00:04:32.487 --> 00:04:34.575
While, in my profession in chess,

00:04:34.575 --> 00:04:36.572
intuition is virtually everything.

00:04:36.572 --> 00:04:39.025
Because chess, some
people don't recognize it,

00:04:39.025 --> 00:04:41.100
is a mathematically infinite game.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.381
The number of moves in the
game of chess, all the moves

00:04:44.381 --> 00:04:48.548
contains 120 zeroes, which is
more than number of seconds

00:04:51.504 --> 00:04:55.087
since the moment Big
Bang created universe.

00:04:56.102 --> 00:04:59.000
So, how can you find your way, you know,

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.024
in this ocean of possibilities?

00:05:01.024 --> 00:05:03.385
And of course, how a
man can fight a machine

00:05:03.385 --> 00:05:07.020
that could calculate
10s and 10s of millions

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:08.683
of positions per second?

00:05:08.683 --> 00:05:13.631
Intuition, because it's all
about decision making process

00:05:13.631 --> 00:05:18.136
and we never employ
calculation as the main tool.

00:05:18.136 --> 00:05:20.407
It's one percent of
calculation, even less,

00:05:20.407 --> 00:05:24.378
and 99% of our
understanding of our ability

00:05:24.378 --> 00:05:28.902
to find intuitive ways
to compare incompatible,

00:05:28.902 --> 00:05:31.996
material verses qualitative,
time verses material.

00:05:31.996 --> 00:05:35.872
So intuition plays a key
role and we have to trust

00:05:35.872 --> 00:05:39.331
our intuition, our instincts,
because in life also

00:05:39.331 --> 00:05:41.079
we have many opportunities where

00:05:41.079 --> 00:05:43.517
we cannot foresee all the consequences

00:05:43.517 --> 00:05:46.587
and there's only one choice,
is just to trust your gut.

00:05:46.587 --> 00:05:49.018
Because when we win, we forget

00:05:49.018 --> 00:05:50.742
that there are a lot of
people who are losing

00:05:50.742 --> 00:05:52.989
and those who are losing
they are analyzing.

00:05:52.989 --> 00:05:56.907
They're trying to find out
the reasons for their loss,

00:05:56.907 --> 00:05:58.940
the mistakes, and if we do nothing,

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:01.729
if we rest on our laurels,

00:06:01.729 --> 00:06:04.236
they'll come back after us next time.

00:06:04.236 --> 00:06:06.355
And I was on top of the
world just for 20 years

00:06:06.355 --> 00:06:08.890
because I was very good, even relentless

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:10.382
in challenging my own success.

00:06:10.382 --> 00:06:12.363
- [Interviewer] And did
you go over all your games?

00:06:12.363 --> 00:06:13.196
- Absolutely

00:06:13.196 --> 00:06:14.105
- [Interviewer] The ones you succeeded

00:06:14.105 --> 00:06:15.508
and the ones you failed?
- Absolutely, yeah.

00:06:15.508 --> 00:06:19.675
Because even the game we won
always contained mistakes,

00:06:21.448 --> 00:06:23.384
inaccuracies, it's inevitable.

00:06:23.384 --> 00:06:26.823
And I can hardly imagine one
Berlin game with no mistakes,

00:06:26.823 --> 00:06:27.996
which means that you won--

00:06:27.996 --> 00:06:28.855
- [Interviewer] On both sides?

00:06:28.855 --> 00:06:30.065
- Yeah, on both sides.

00:06:30.065 --> 00:06:30.898
- [Interviewer] But the trick is noticing

00:06:30.898 --> 00:06:32.199
when the other guy makes a mistake.

00:06:32.199 --> 00:06:33.032
- Absolutely, yeah.

00:06:33.032 --> 00:06:36.034
If we won, it's most
likely because our opponent

00:06:36.034 --> 00:06:39.471
made their last mistake,
but he made the last mistake

00:06:39.471 --> 00:06:41.424
and he's definitely analyzing the game

00:06:41.424 --> 00:06:43.174
and he'll find what we did wrong

00:06:43.174 --> 00:06:44.907
in the previous stage of the game,

00:06:44.907 --> 00:06:46.925
so what is important is to find it first

00:06:46.925 --> 00:06:48.439
and to make sure that next time

00:06:48.439 --> 00:06:50.625
when he's trying to actually
surprise us with something

00:06:50.625 --> 00:06:52.579
we'll be one step ahead.

00:06:52.579 --> 00:06:57.075
I made the conscious
decision that Linares 2005

00:06:57.075 --> 00:06:58.476
will be my last professional

00:06:58.476 --> 00:07:02.508
and today I played my
last professional game.

00:07:02.508 --> 00:07:06.675
- Is there a maximum age, or
can you be a chess champion

00:07:09.107 --> 00:07:11.796
if you want to be at 65?

00:07:11.796 --> 00:07:13.062
- It's a very demanding game,

00:07:13.062 --> 00:07:15.565
so you have to spend time at the computer

00:07:15.565 --> 00:07:18.205
analyzing, preparing new ideas

00:07:18.205 --> 00:07:20.577
and then you just play
under this heavy pressure,

00:07:20.577 --> 00:07:23.744
so it sucks a lot of energy out of you

00:07:26.925 --> 00:07:31.063
and players in their 20s or
early 30s, they definitely

00:07:31.063 --> 00:07:34.960
have a physical advantage
over older competitors.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:38.606
I probably would be able
to play a few more years,

00:07:38.606 --> 00:07:41.388
but I made a decision to retire

00:07:41.388 --> 00:07:43.400
and it was not just overnight, you know,

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:45.302
at the end of the tournament in Spain

00:07:45.302 --> 00:07:48.892
in the city of Linares, but
just beforehand I thought

00:07:48.892 --> 00:07:53.140
I already made enough
difference in the game of chess.

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:54.585
So just making a difference was important,

00:07:54.585 --> 00:07:58.322
and I just recognized that
maybe it's time just to move on.

00:07:58.322 --> 00:08:03.180
Just to start changing my
life before it's too late.

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:06.711
You can't stay in chess indefinitely,

00:08:06.711 --> 00:08:09.876
because everybody needs challenges

00:08:09.876 --> 00:08:12.371
and I thought about other challenges.

00:08:12.371 --> 00:08:16.059
Is there any universal recipe for success?

00:08:16.059 --> 00:08:17.523
I don't think so.

00:08:17.523 --> 00:08:20.335
I think we're all unique
and it all depends on us,

00:08:20.335 --> 00:08:23.446
what kind of ingredients, and
they're similar ingredients,

00:08:23.446 --> 00:08:27.113
we'd like to put into
our own magic formula.

00:08:28.344 --> 00:08:32.177
When we look at the
examples from the history,

00:08:33.527 --> 00:08:35.238
look at the success stories.

00:08:35.238 --> 00:08:38.512
We could find a different
definition of success.

00:08:38.512 --> 00:08:40.607
For instance, Mikhail Botvinnik,

00:08:40.607 --> 00:08:45.267
the sixth world champion
known for his notoriety

00:08:45.267 --> 00:08:48.239
and called the patriarch
of the Soviet chess school.

00:08:48.239 --> 00:08:51.794
Probably the greatest scientist
in the history of chess.

00:08:51.794 --> 00:08:55.961
So, his formula was very solid,
and very much the ground.

00:08:57.148 --> 00:09:01.074
Talent, good health, strong
nerves, fighting spirit,

00:09:01.074 --> 00:09:03.657
and good work ethic, it worked.

00:09:05.267 --> 00:09:07.767
Thomas Edison was even duller.

00:09:09.917 --> 00:09:14.084
He said success is 1% inspiration
and 99% of perspiration.

00:09:17.659 --> 00:09:21.519
But just look at the
different side of equation.

00:09:21.519 --> 00:09:25.050
Alexander Pushkin, the
greatest Russian poet,

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:28.530
considered to be the father of
the modern Russian language.

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:31.116
It's the man who with the
lightest touch of genius

00:09:31.116 --> 00:09:35.267
could produce the best pieces
of Russian literature ever.

00:09:35.267 --> 00:09:38.800
He was a man of joy, he was a gambler.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:41.846
He was even a rake, always
lived in the red ink.

00:09:41.846 --> 00:09:43.607
So his formula is quite different.

00:09:43.607 --> 00:09:47.524
A genius, luck, and
experience, it also worked.

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:50.264
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:09:50.264 --> 00:09:52.846
I made this video because
one of you asked me to,

00:09:52.846 --> 00:09:54.488
so if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:09:54.488 --> 00:09:56.363
that you want me to profile next,

00:09:56.363 --> 00:09:58.996
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:09:58.996 --> 00:10:02.755
I'd also to love which of
Garry Kasparov's top ten rules

00:10:02.755 --> 00:10:05.788
meant the most to you, leave
it in the comments below

00:10:05.788 --> 00:10:07.034
and I'll join in the discussion.

00:10:07.034 --> 00:10:09.140
Thank you so much for
watching, continue to believe,

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:10.728
and I'll see you soon.

00:10:10.728 --> 00:10:13.119
(wind rushing)
(basketball bouncing)

